are the magnitude of the loss in the negative outcome. Since the reason focuses on the sum of outcome differences, and both lotteries have a zero outcome, the only difference is in the negative outcome. The difference between the outcomes is 3000 (6000 - 3000). The sum of pairwise differences is not clearly defined, but if we interpret it as the total variation in losses, then the difference is 3000. The reason would prefer the lottery with the smaller loss in the adverse case — which is B. So the reason, when applied, would prefer B because the loss is smaller in the event of a loss. Thus, the decision reason can be applied: it compares the losses and prefers the one with the smaller loss in the adverse outcome, which is B. The sum of pairwise differences (in absolute value) is 3000, and since this is the only difference, the reason applies to the decision problem.",
